<div>
  <div class="learn-block">
    <p class="learn-title">
      Halt: Pause, reset, the start and end of every pattern
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span>The ribcage is the horse’s accordion
      — when it expands and contracts freely, the whole body moves with ease.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Walk -->
    <p class="learn-title">Walk: The First Breath of Bend</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span> Natural swing, lateral softness,
      and the horse’s willingness to follow the inside leg.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A loose ribcage creates a rolling, four-beat walk.</li>
      <li>Tight ribs make the walk short, flat, or mechanical.</li>
      <li>Horses with emotional tension often hold their breath here.</li>
      <li>True bend begins with ribcage softness — not the neck.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Trot -->
    <p class="learn-title">Trot: Rhythm Shaped by the Ribs</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span> Symmetry, elasticity, and the
      horse’s ability to bend through the body.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Loose ribs allow the trot to swing and lift.</li>
      <li>Tight ribs create a stiff, sewing-machine trot.</li>
      <li>Ribcage restriction often shows as drifting or falling in.</li>
      <li>Diagonal pairs move more freely when the ribs can expand.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Canter -->
    <p class="learn-title">Canter: The Coil of the Ribcage</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span> Balance, coordination, and the
      horse’s ability to organize the body.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Loose ribs allow the horse to “coil the spring” for canter lift.</li>
      <li>Tight ribs cause rushing, leaning, or difficulty holding leads.</li>
      <li>True canter bend comes from the ribs, not the neck.</li>
      <li>Emotional tension often shows as a high head and hollow back.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Gallop -->
    <p class="learn-title">Gallop: Breath, Freedom & Expansion</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span> How fully the horse can expand the
      ribcage for power and speed.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Loose ribs = long, ground-covering stride.</li>
      <li>Tight ribs = short, choppy, energy-wasting gallop.</li>
      <li>Horses with past trauma often brace here.</li>
      <li>Breath and ribcage expansion drive the entire gait.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Bend & Flexion -->
    <p class="learn-title">Bend: The Ribcage as the Center of Flexion</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">When the ribcage is soft:</span> The horse bends
      through the body, not just the neck.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Inside leg creates true bend through the ribs.</li>
      <li>Shoulders stay aligned instead of falling in.</li>
      <li>Hindquarters step under instead of drifting out.</li>
      <li>Neck softness becomes a reflection of rib softness.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Emotional Influence -->
    <p class="learn-title">Emotional Influence on Ribcage Movement</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">Reveals:</span> The horse’s internal state — calm,
      stressed, guarded, or willing.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Relaxed horses breathe deeply and swing through the ribs.</li>
      <li>Stressed horses hold their breath and tighten the ribcage.</li>
      <li>Previously abused horses may brace or freeze through the ribs.</li>
      <li>Soft ribs = soft mind = soft movement.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="learn-block">
    <!-- Whole System -->
    <p class="learn-title">The Whole System: Ribcage Freedom = Fluid Gaits</p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">When the ribcage is free:</span> The horse bends,
      breathes, and moves with harmony.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="emphasis">When the ribcage is tight:</span> The horse braces,
      rushes, or loses rhythm.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Rib softness shapes every gait</li>
      <li>Breath drives rhythm and relaxation</li>
      <li>Bend begins in the ribs, not the neck</li>
      <li>Fluid movement requires emotional and physical freedom</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
